        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.817,
            0.076,
            0.904,
            0.13
          ],
          "content": "Math Talk"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.092,
            0.801,
            0.131
          ],
          "content": "2. Create your own number system of base 4, and represent numbers from 1 to 16."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.14,
            0.848,
            0.179
          ],
          "content": "3. Give a simple rule to multiply a given number by 5 in the base-5 system that we created."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.208,
            0.586,
            0.233
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.4. Place Value Representation"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.247,
            0.605,
            0.27
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "I. The Mesopotamian Number System"
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.174,
            0.289,
            0.852,
            0.518
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [],
          "image_source": "images/page_22_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.532,
            0.848,
            0.608
          ],
          "content": "In the beginning, the number system used in ancient Mesopotamia had different symbols for different landmark numbers. In later times, it became a base-60 system, also called the **sexagesimal system**, with a very efficient number representation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.61,
            0.849,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "It has puzzled many why they chose base 60. Different theories exist to explain this, ranging from the connection between 60 and the periods of some important events (like the length of their lunar month which had 30 days, or the time taken for the Sun to complete one revolution around the Earth when Earth is taken to be stationary), the ease of representing fractions (we will not go into this here), their earlier sequence of landmark numbers — 1, 10, 60, 600, 3600, 36000,… — getting reduced to only the powers of 60, and so on."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.758,
            0.848,
            0.837
          ],
          "content": "The influence of the Mesopotamian sexagesimal system, also known as the **Babylonian number system**, can be seen even now in our units of time measurements—1 hour = 60 minutes and 1 minute = 60 seconds. This system used the symbol for 1 and for 10."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.836,
            0.848,
            0.893
          ],
          "content": "Let us now briefly pause on the study of their number system, and ideate on how one can build an efficient number system using the Mesopotamian features seen so far."
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.085,
            0.915,
            0.118,
            0.936
          ],
          "content": "70"
        },
